TwoDrive Debian packaging preparation
====================================

This package is not yet ready for Debian archive submission. It builds the
existing GNOME OneDrive client. The Flutter Development UI is not included.

Build with scripts/build-deb.sh on Debian/Ubuntu, after provisioning Rust >=
1.91, Cargo, debhelper 13, dh-sequence-python3, pkg-config, libsqlite3-dev,
python3-gi and gir1.2-gtk-4.0. In this upstream preparation phase only, run
`cargo fetch --locked` BEFORE the package build to populate the Cargo cache.
debian/rules always builds/tests offline; a missing dependency fails the build.
Use `dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc` to build directly. Artifacts contain generated
ELF dependencies, root-owned files, manuals, copyright and a Debian changelog.
SQLite comes from the system development package, not the bundled C source.
User services are not enabled or started by installation.

Before an official upload:
* Package/audit the locked Rust dependencies in Debian, declare their exact
  librust-*-dev Build-Depends, and use the /usr/share/cargo/registry directory
  source with dh-cargo. A developer's Cargo cache is NOT an archive build input.
  Ring and other native dependency convenience copies also need review.
* Audit copyright/license attribution for all source assets and linked Rust
  dependencies; generate the required binary-package license material. The
  current DEP-5 file describes this repository's own code, not every crate.
* Confirm a reachable maintainer identity/email and obtain a Debian sponsor.
* Create an ITP, prepare the original source tarball plus quilt Debian changes,
  run clean offline sbuild, autopkgtest and piuparts on Debian unstable, and
  resolve Lintian findings. The upstream CI is not a substitute for sbuild.
* Only after review change UNRELEASED to unstable and sign the source upload.

For a source package, place an upstream tree (without debian/ or generated
build outputs) in ../twodrive_0.2.14.orig.tar.xz, then run
`dpkg-buildpackage -S -us -uc`. Do not re-use an original tarball with differing
contents under the same upstream version. The upstream tag v0.2.14-1 includes
a packaging suffix; Debian's upstream version here remains 0.2.14.

The Flutter SDK/engine and Development iroh dependency closure require their
own distribution packaging work before a Flutter desktop package could enter
the Debian archive. Neither Microsoft Store certification nor apt repository
acceptance is implied by these build scripts.
